k  in earnest and now I had computers so  now I was really Damon and so I got  hired by a big pension fund in the Twin  Cities called control data it was an old  conglomerate and back then you know the  dark ages really were dark a lot of  people didn't even know what style their  portfolio manager was using were they  growth were they value where they growth  at a reasonable price you know all of  these things and they said we want you  to come in and make some sense of this  for us so I founded the company of  Shaughnessy Capital Management in 1987  to do that and we created what were  called normal portfolios they're like  highly specialized benchmarks it's not  like using the S&amp;P 500 or using the  Russell 1 value you know as a kind of a  general benchmark for how manager might  be doing what we did was take the  manager's portfolios put them on a  database look at the most extreme  differences in their portfolio from that  universe and then use the most extreme  differences to screen the same universe  for a portfolio of stocks that looked  acted and most importantly performed  like the underlying manager the whole  purpose of a normal portfolio was really  simple it was is this manager through  his or her activity trading selling  buying adding any significant value to  their underlying stat style or is it all  the style hmm well what we found was my  kind of aha moment even though we  attributed huge transaction costs to the  portfolio's that we created as the  normals they killed their underlying  managers and I don't mean just by a  percent or two they absolutely destroyed  them why  because this was the inkling of I wonder  if that cartoon by Kelly is right Pogo  we've met the enemy and it's us and  that's when I started to develop the  theory that basically human beings are  designed to suck as investors right so  we are optimized towards a history that  no longer exists and hasn't existed in  eons right when we're out walking on the  Savannah and we see a bush rustling it's  a probably a very good idea to turn  around and run like hell right because  you don't know what that is it could be  a predator it could be something else  but you're not gonna take your chances  you're just gonna run well all of our  ancestors of everyone sitting in this  room ran so the ones who didn't run the  braver all got killed and so that that  made us optimized to be very very  fearful of perceived threats and so when  markets as we know are incredibly  volatile what do people do the amygdala  takes over the fear takes over the brain  and there's almost nothing you can do to  fight it unless you are a quantitative  investor who does not override their  model right so I started reading a lot  of what is now known as behavioral  finance and actually I found a book by a  guy by the name of Robin Dawes Britt who  his book was actually about  psychotherapy but he had a great chapter  about all of the studies that had been  done over actuarial outcomes ie  algorithmic outcomes and clinical  outcomes that means I'm the doctor  diagnosing you and when they started all  these things in the 50s they all thought  all of the researchers thought that the  human expert was going to soar above  these simple algorithmic solutions the  algorithmic solutions destroyed them and  they destroyed them for one reason and  one reason only they were consistently  applied  time and time and time again we humans  are far more interesting right we get  hung over we have a fight with our  spouse we're in a bad mood or we're in a  good mood and we let that influence our  decisions in financial markets you know  there's a study that just sticks out in  my mind and that is about people trying  to get parole if you want to get per  mole per roll make sure that you are one  of the first appointments of the day the  people who are at the you know the eight  to ten in their hearing they get parole  at at something like eighty five percent  of the time  guess who almo